---
/**
 * About, English. The third register of the site, and the only informal one.
 *
 * Four vetoes apply only here (information-architecture.md section 4.5):
 * no third parties even though Bits&Roses is co-run with his partner, nothing
 * a recruiter could only weigh against him, care with what a personal photo
 * reveals, and informal is not the same as without judgement. It is also the
 * page where a job search most easily leaks in, which is why the CTA at the
 * bottom is the one to read twice.
 *
 * The photo lives in src/assets rather than public/, so Astro generates the
 * responsive variants and hashes the filename at build time. It is a copy of
 * web/content/assets/franc-and-cosmo.jpg, EXIF already stripped there: the
 * original HEIC carried GPS, and this repository is public.
 *
 * WIDTH, changed 2026-08-08 on Franc's call. The body spans the full shell
 * instead of sitting in a centred 760px column. With no side index there is
 * nothing in the left gutter, so a centred column reads as crooked against the
 * hero, which is left-aligned.
 *
 * ⚠️ It costs the measure: at 1072px the line runs 95 to 100 characters, and
 * adr-002 section 6.4 set 760 precisely to keep it at 65 to 70. Taken with that
 * on the table, because this page is getting more images and the text will end
 * up in columns beside them. Revisit if it does not.
 *
 * The photo keeps 760, now as an explicit cap on the image rather than one
 * inherited from a wrapper.
 *
 * Copy transcribed verbatim from web/content/en/about.md.
 */
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHeader from '../components/PageHeader.astro';
import portrait from '../assets/franc-and-cosmo.jpg';
---

<BaseLayout locale="en" current="about" title="About | Franc Gaya" description="From mechanic to electronics engineer, a rescue dog called Cosmo, and a web side project that happens to have built this site.">
  <!-- No lead here on purpose: this is the one page whose first block is the
       photo, and whose opening line belongs to the first section. Moving that
       line up into the band would read better and would put it above the
       photo, which is not the order the content is written in. -->
  <PageHeader>
    <Fragment slot="title">About</Fragment>
  </PageHeader>

  <div class="mx-auto max-w-shell px-6 pt-16">
    <div class="space-y-20">
      <Image
        src={portrait}
        alt="Franc and Cosmo"
        widths={[480, 760, 1520]}
        sizes="(min-width: 800px) 760px, 100vw"
        class="w-full max-w-prose rounded-card"
        loading="eager"
      />

      <section>
        <div class="section-rule" aria-hidden="true"></div>
        <h2 class="mt-5 text-h2 text-heading">From mechanic to engineer</h2>
        <div class="prose-block mt-6 text-ink">
          <p class="text-lead">I started working at eighteen, in a workshop, with my hands.</p>
          <p>For a while that was the entire plan. I took a vocational degree in automotive technology, worked on cars, then on Barcelona's street-cleaning fleet, then on refrigeration units for refrigerated trucks. Then I enrolled at university and did both at once for four years: workshop in the morning, lectures in the afternoon, and whatever was left of the day for studying. I came out of it with the best academic record of my year, which I am mentioning exactly once, here, because the sentence takes about two seconds to read and the thing itself took four years.</p>
          <p>There is a joke buried in all that which I did not plan. I spent a year as a refrigeration mechanic, servicing compressors and evaporators on trucks. Almost twenty years later I am the engineer responsible for the electronics of a line of refrigerators. I would love to claim that was a strategy. It was not.</p>
          <p>What did carry across is the part that has nothing to do with refrigeration: I am comfortable with the physical object. I will happily modify a market appliance to fit my own electronics into it, build a rig with real loads and sensors, or open something up rather than argue about what it might be doing.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section>
        <div class="section-rule" aria-hidden="true"></div>
        <h2 class="mt-5 text-h2 text-heading">Outside work</h2>
        <div class="prose-block mt-6 text-ink">
          <p>There is a pattern to how I spend my time off and I am not going to pretend otherwise. I like systems I can measure and adjust.</p>
          <p><strong>Cosmo.</strong> He is a rescue, currently eight months old, a mix of bodeguero and dachshund in harlequin black and white, and he came home at two months. He has quietly reorganized my weekends around beaches, dog parks, and routes I would never otherwise have walked, and I read a great deal more about canine training and behavior than anybody strictly needs to.</p>
          <p><strong>Cooking</strong>, approached as a discipline rather than a hobby: temperatures, times, textures, and equipment. It is not a coincidence that I work on ovens, and it is genuinely useful to have cooked on the thing you are writing the control algorithm for.</p>
          <p><strong>Inline skating</strong>, where I have somehow ended up tuning boot fit, insoles, and wheel setup with the same energy I put into a calibration session at work. The equipment is a system too.</p>
          <p><strong>Electronics that never becomes a product</strong>, which is the one that keeps the job feeling like the hobby it started as: microcontrollers on a desk, connected things around the house, and the occasional vehicle fault I would rather diagnose myself than describe to somebody else.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- The credit is a demonstration rather than a claim: the site being
           read is the evidence. It is also the one place on the site that has
           to say what it is NOT, and it says so about a side project rather
           than about the role, which is why writing rule 5 is not breached. -->
      <section>
        <div class="section-rule" aria-hidden="true"></div>
        <h2 class="mt-5 text-h2 text-heading">Bits&amp;Roses</h2>
        <div class="prose-block mt-6 text-ink">
          <p>This site was built by Bits&amp;Roses, a side project I co-founded in 2012 with other people.</p>
          <p>Around eight websites in fourteen years, on demand, with long quiet stretches in between. It is not a parallel career and I am not selling myself as a web developer: it is where the part of me that likes building things gets to build something that is not embedded, on a timescale measured in weeks rather than years. Every so often that is exactly what I need.</p>
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
